     tions of the callback.  Note, that currently the number of initial ticks
     silently may be set identical to the number of ticks between callback in‐
     vocations.  The function returns a timer identifier that can be used to
     stop the timer via ttiimmeerr__ssttoopp().  If a module is unloaded all timers
     started by the module that have not expired yet are stopped.

   FFIILLEE DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTOORR SSUUPPPPOORRTT
     A module may need to get input from socket file descriptors without
     blocking the daemon (for example to implement alternative SNMP trans‐
     ports).

     The function ffdd__sseelleecctt() causes the callback function _f_u_n_c to be called
     with the file descriptor _f_d and the user argument _u_a_r_g whenever the file
     descriptor _f_d can be read or has a close condition.  If the file descrip‐
     tor is not in non-blocking mode, it is set to non-blocking mode.  If the
     callback is not needed anymore, ffdd__ddeesseelleecctt() may be called with the
     value returned from ffdd__sseelleecctt().  All file descriptors selected by a mod‐
     ule are automatically deselected when the module is unloaded.

     To temporarily suspend the file descriptor registration ffdd__ssuussppeenndd() can
     be called.  This also causes the file descriptor to be switched back to
     blocking mode if it was blocking prior the call to ffdd__sseelleecctt().  This is
     necessary to do synchronous input on a selected socket.  The effect of
     ffdd__ssuussppeenndd() can be undone with ffdd__rreessuummee().

   OOBBJJEECCTT RREESSOOUURRCCEESS
     The system group contains an object resource table.  A module may create
     an entry in this table by calling oorr__rreeggiisstteerr() with the _o_i_d to be regis‐
     tered, a textual description in _s_t_r and a pointer to the module _m_o_d.  The
     registration can be removed with oorr__uunnrreeggiisstteerr().  All registrations of a
     module are automatically removed if the module is unloaded.

   TTRRAANNSSMMIITT AANNDD RREECCEEIIVVEE BBUUFFFFEERRSS
     A buffer is allocated via bbuuff__aalllloocc().  The argument must be 1 for trans‐
     mit and 0 for receive buffers.  The function may return NULL if there is
     no memory available.  The current buffersize can be obtained with
     bbuuff__ssiizzee().

PPRROOCCEESSSSIINNGG PPDDUUSS
     For modules that need to do their own PDU processing (for example for
     proxying) the following functions are available:

     Function ssnnmmpp__iinnppuutt__ssttaarrtt() decodes the PDU, searches the community, and
     sets the global _t_h_i_s___t_i_c_k.  It returns one of the following error codes:

     SNMPD_INPUT_OK         Everything ok, continue with processing.

     SNMPD_INPUT_FAILED     The PDU could not be decoded, has a wrong version
                            or an unknown community string.

     SNMPD_INPUT_VALBADLEN  A SET PDU had a value field in a binding with a
                            wrong length field in an ASN.1 header.

     SNMPD_INPUT_VALRANGE   A SET PDU had a value field in a binding with a
                            value that is out of range for the given ASN.1
                            type.

     SNMPD_INPUT_VALBADENC  A SET PDU had a value field in a binding with
                            wrong ASN.1 encoding.

     SNMPD_INPUT_TRUNC      The buffer appears to contain a valid begin of a
                            PDU, but is too short.  For streaming transports
                            this means that the caller must save what he al‐
                            ready has and trying to obtain more input and
                            reissue this input to the function.  For datagram
                            transports this means that part of the datagram
                            was lost and the input should be ignored.

     The function ssnnmmpp__iinnppuutt__ffiinniisshh() does the other half of processing: if
     ssnnmmpp__iinnppuutt__ssttaarrtt() did not return OK, tries to construct an error re‐
     sponse.  If the start was OK, it calls the correct function from
     bsnmpagent(3) to execute the request and depending on the outcome con‐
     structs a response or error response PDU or ignores the request PDU.  It
     returns either SNMPD_INPUT_OK or SNMPD_INPUT_FAILED.  In the first case a
     response PDU was constructed and should be sent.

     The function ssnnmmpp__oouuttppuutt() takes a PDU and encodes it.

     The function ssnnmmpp__sseenndd__ppoorrtt() takes a PDU, encodes it and sends it
     through the given port (identified by the transport and the index in the
     port table) to the given address.

     The function ssnnmmpp__sseenndd__ttrraapp() sends a trap to all trap destinations.  The
     arguments are the _o_i_d identifying the trap and a NULL-terminated list of
     _s_t_r_u_c_t _s_n_m_p___v_a_l_u_e pointers that are to be inserted into the trap binding
     list.  ssnnmmpp__ppdduu__aauutthh__aacccceessss() verifies whether access to the object IDs
     contained in the _p_d_u
      should be granted or denied, according to the configured View-Based Ac‐
     cess rules.  _i_p contains the index of the first varbinding to which ac‐
     cess was denied, or 0 if access to all varbindings in the PDU is granted.

   SSIIMMPPLLEE AACCTTIIOONN SSUUPPPPOORRTT
     For simple scalar variables that need no dependencies a number of support
     functions is available to handle the set, commit, rollback and get.

     The following functions are used for OCTET STRING scalars, either NUL
     terminated or not:

     ssttrriinngg__ssaavvee()
                should be called for SNMP_OP_SET.  _v_a_l_u_e and _c_t_x are the resp.
                arguments to the node callback.  _v_a_l_p is a pointer to the
                pointer that holds the current value and _r_e_q___s_i_z_e should be -1
                if any size of the string is acceptable or a number larger or
                equal zero if the string must have a specific size.  The func‐
                tion saves the old value in the scratch area (note, that any
                initial value must have been allocated by malloc(3)), allo‐
                cates a new string, copies over the new value, NUL-terminates
                it and sets the new current value.

     ssttrriinngg__ccoommmmiitt()
                simply frees the saved old value in the scratch area.

     ssttrriinngg__rroollllbbaacckk()
                frees the new value, and puts back the old one.

     ssttrriinngg__ggeett()
                is used for GET or GETNEXT.  The function

     ssttrriinngg__ggeett__mmaaxx()
                can be used instead of ssttrriinngg__ggeett() to ensure that the re‐
                turned string has a certain maximum length.  If _l_e_n is -1, the
                length is computed via strlen(3) from the current string
                value.  If the current value is NULL, a OCTET STRING of zero
                length is returned.

     ssttrriinngg__ffrreeee()
                must be called if either rollback or commit fails to free the
                saved old value.

     The following functions are used to process scalars of type IP-address:

     iipp__ssaavvee()  Saves the current value in the scratch area and sets the new
                value from _v_a_l_p.

     iipp__ccoommmmiitt()
                Does nothing.

     iipp__rroollllbbaacckk()
                Restores the old IP address from the scratch area.

     iipp__ggeett()   Retrieves the IP current address.

     The following functions handle OID-typed variables:

     ooiidd__ssaavvee()
                Saves the current value in the scratch area by allocating a
                _s_t_r_u_c_t _a_s_n___o_i_d with malloc(3) and sets the new value from _o_i_d.

     ooiidd__ccoommmmiitt()
